+/**
+ * PATCH: what a paragraph says, for the times there is no file to play it.
+ *
+ * PlaybackQueue falls back to the device's speech engine when a paragraph's
+ * audio cannot be fetched, and the engine needs the text. It cannot ask
+ * JavaScript for it: the fallback matters most while the app is in the
+ * background, which is exactly when the WebView's JavaScript engine has
+ * stopped running (see PlaybackQueue's header). Sending the text down with
+ * every queue instead would put roughly 1.5 MB of Thai across the bridge on
+ * every press of play, charged to every listener, nearly all of whom never
+ * lose their connection.
+ *
+ * So the text ships inside the app, in assets/public/data/speech-<book>.json,
+ * generated by scripts/tts-render/speechText.mjs from the same function that
+ * decided what to render and named every audio file. It is keyed by that hash
+ * — the basename of the entry's URL — because the URL is the only identifier a
+ * queue entry carries, and because the two spellings of a sub-clause label
+ * hash apart, which lets one file serve every voice.
+ *
+ * Loaded lazily, one book at a time, and only when a paragraph has actually
+ * failed: a listener who never loses signal never pays for any of it.
+ */
